  "shorts": [
    {
      "short_id": "short259",
      "angle": "The process servers knew children removed posted writs, but the record does not say that happened to these three tenants.",
      "avoids": "No composite people, invented aftermath, guessed vote, or claim that removal occurred in these tenants' cases.",
      "funnel_question_left_for_longform": "If a paper on the door is unreliable, what notice does due process require instead?",
      "loop": true,
      "loop_join": "The final line returns to the process servers whose own testimony opens the Short.",
      "era": "Louisville, Kentucky, early 1980s",
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "L1",
          "text": "The men who taped eviction notices to doors were asked under oath whether children pulled them down. One answered: they had plenty of trouble.",
          "delivery": "intense",
          "source_lines": [
            "The record was made of depositions from the process servers themselves."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L2",
          "text": "He said he had seen children do it at Village West. That testimony was about the servers' experience, not proof about these three tenants' doors.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "One deposition in that footnote records the exchange."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L3",
          "text": "The three tenants said they never saw the summonses and learned of the cases only after default judgments and writs of possession.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "They claim never to have seen these posted summonses."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L4",
          "text": "The Court said posted notices were removed often enough that the papers failed before they could do the job notice was supposed to do.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "The questioner checked it. You had trouble? With kids, yeah. Yeah."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L5",
          "text": "It did not say paper on a door always fails. In many situations, the Court said, posting can be appropriate and effective.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "None of which, on its own, makes posting unconstitutional."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L6",
          "text": "But on this record, merely posting on an apartment door did not meet the minimum standards of due process.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "Then the turn."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L7",
          "text": "The tenants had already lost by default and lost their chance to appeal before, they said, they knew proceedings existed.",
          "delivery": "building",
          "source_lines": [
            "They claim never to have seen these posted summonses."
          ],
          "claims": []
        },
        {
          "id": "L8",
          "text": "The ruling began with what the men posting the papers already knew: sometimes the notice did not stay where they put it.",
          "delivery": "calm",
          "source_lines": [
            "The record was made of depositions from the process servers themselves."
          ],
          "claims": []
        }
      ],
